 {
  "topic": "Optimizing iterutils.research for complex object graphs",
  "turns": [
   {
    "role": "user",
    "text": "Hey Bob, I'm working with `iterutils.research` on some really gnarly object graphs we get from our parsing layer. It's definitely powerful, but I'm running into some perf issues, especially when objects reference each other cyclically or share many common sub-graphs."
   },
   {
    "role": "assistant",
    "text": "Ah, the classic `research` challenge! You might want to check out the `cache` parameter (added in 1737473). It's designed specifically for those scenarios, memoizing results for already visited objects to prevent redundant processing and also handle cycles gracefully."
   },
   {
    "role": "user",
    "text": "Right, I remember seeing that commit! So if I set `cache=True`, does it cache the *return* of the `predicate` for a given node, or does it cache the fact that it *traversed* that node to avoid re-entering it?"
   },
   {
    "role": "assistant",
    "text": "It's the latter: it tracks visited object instances by their `id()`. So if `research` encounters the same object again (same memory address), it'll skip re-applying the predicate or re-traversing its children. It's less about caching predicate *results* and more about preventing redundant graph traversal. This also helps `remap` avoid infinite loops."
   },
   {
    "role": "user",
    "text": "Okay, that makes a lot of sense for my current problem \u2013 I have many shared custom objects. Speaking of custom objects, some of my graph nodes aren't just dicts or lists; they're custom classes with their own internal `children` attributes. `research` isn't diving into them correctly."
   },
   {
    "role": "assistant",
    "text": "For that, you'll need the `enter` parameter, which came in with 1f07518. You can pass a callable to `enter` that `research` will use to determine how to traverse *into* any given object. It gets `(path, key, val)` and should yield `(new_key, new_val)` pairs for whatever children you want it to explore."
   },
   {
    "role": "user",
    "text": "So, I could write an `enter` function that checks `isinstance(val, MyCustomNode)` and if so, yields `('children', val.children)` or iterates over `val.edges`? That sounds perfect for explicitly defining traversal logic."
   },
   {
    "role": "assistant",
    "text": "Exactly. It completely overrides how `research` explores a node. You could even use other `iterutils` functions within your `enter` callable, like `partition`, if you wanted to conditionally yield different types of children based on some predicate (e.g., only traverse 'active' child nodes)."
   },
   {
    "role": "user",
    "text": "That's a powerful combination! So, if I use `enter` to correctly expose the internal structure of my custom objects, and `cache=True` to prevent re-processing, I should be able to efficiently navigate my entire graph."
   },
   {
    "role": "assistant",
    "text": "Yep, that's the general strategy. Just be careful with your `enter` function; it effectively defines your graph structure for `research`. Any subtle bugs there can lead to missed nodes or incorrect traversal. The tests for `enter` in `test_iterutils.py` are a good place to see examples."
   },
   {
    "role": "user",
    "text": "Will do. Thanks for the detailed explanation, Bob! This clarifies a lot."
   }
  ],
  "id": "decoy-test_iterutils-11"
 },
