 {
  "topic": "Refactoring `is_file_like` post-Python 2 and type hinting",
  "turns": [
   {
    "role": "user",
    "text": "Hey, I was digging through `ioutils.py` today for a bit, specifically around how we handle file-like objects. The `is_file_like` check feels a little, I don't know, broad? Even after all the Py2 cleanup."
   },
   {
    "role": "assistant",
    "text": "Yeah, that's an area with quite a history. After we dropped Python 2.6 and 2.7 support with commit `a8fad5e`, we were able to strip out a lot of the `file` built-in and `StringIO`/`BytesIO` specific checks that were needed for Py2. It certainly made the core logic much simpler, focusing more on `read`/`write`/`seek` methods. What's making it feel broad to you?"
   },
   {
    "role": "user",
    "text": "Well, it primarily uses `hasattr` for `read`, `write`, `seek`, etc. Which is fine for duck typing, but I've run into cases where an object might have a `read` attribute, but it's a no-op or raises `NotImplementedError` when called. `hasattr` alone doesn't guarantee actual functionality. Like, should we be more explicit about readable vs. writable file-likes?"
   },
   {
    "role": "assistant",
    "text": "That's a valid concern. Historically, `boltons` has leaned heavily into generous duck-typing for `is_file_like` to accommodate a wide variety of stream-like objects. The subsequent `pyupgrade --py3-plus` and `--py37-plus` runs (`675afd8`, `7bd0d48`) primarily cleaned up syntax, but didn't fundamentally alter that behavioral contract. What we did heavily invest in with `59dc6be` was adding more precise type hints, like `typing.TextIO` or `typing.BinaryIO`, which are stricter for static analysis on the *caller's* side."
   },
   {
    "role": "user",
    "text": "I see. So the type hints are really meant to shift some of that validation responsibility to the static analysis tools, rather than changing the runtime behavior of `is_file_like` itself. So for a utility function like `ioutils.in_chunks`, it expects a truly readable stream, but `is_file_like` is just a first pass."
   },
   {
    "role": "assistant",
    "text": "Precisely. For `in_chunks`, the expectation is that if `is_file_like` returns true, it's a *practically* readable stream. To enforce runtime strictness beyond `hasattr`, we'd have to try calling methods (e.g., `obj.read(0)`), which adds overhead and potential side-effects. Given `boltons`' role as a general utility, that level of runtime validation has typically been left to the consuming application, or handled through more specific wrappers if needed."
   },
   {
    "role": "user",
    "text": "That makes sense for a library with such broad utility goals. I guess my mental model was skewed by needing a stricter guarantee in a specific context. I should probably add an explicit runtime check in my own code if `ioutils.is_file_like` passes, but I *really* need to confirm it's callable and functional for `read` or `write`."
   },
   {
    "role": "assistant",
    "text": "Exactly. Or, if you're writing new code, leverage the type annotations more heavily. If your function expects `typing.BinaryIO`, type checkers will flag it if you pass in something that only has a `read` method but isn't a proper `IOBase` subclass or doesn't implement the full protocol. That's where a lot of the effort in `59dc6be` really pays off."
   },
   {
    "role": "user",
    "text": "Good point. I often forget to let the type checker do more of the heavy lifting. So, `is_file_like` is more of a 'does it have the shape?' rather than 'does it reliably perform?' check, and the stronger guarantees are left for higher-level type hints or explicit checks."
   },
   {
    "role": "assistant",
    "text": "That's a perfect summary. It's about providing a useful, broadly applicable check without imposing overly strict runtime costs, letting downstream consumers refine as needed using Python's type system or their own explicit validations."
   }
  ],
  "id": "decoy-ioutils-15"
 },
